Differential Cartography of Large Language Model Behavior: Mathematical Foundations, Proofs, and Experimental Architecture

Version 3.0 — Consolidated Theoretical Framework for PhD Research

---

Abstract

We formalize a research program for the reconstruction of latent functional properties of a black-box large language model (LLM)  f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}  from its responses to families of controlled, semantically preserved perturbations. We abandon the naïve continuous interpolation  X(t) = (1-t)X_0 + tX_1  — inapplicable to a discrete prompt space — in favor of a perturbation graph  \mathcal{G} = (V, E, W) . Three formal objects structure the program: the behavioral displacement field  \mathcal{B} = \{(P_{ij}, B_{ij}, K_{ij}, \tau_{ij})\}_{(i,j) \in E} , the local sensitivity  K_{ij} = B_{ij} / P_{ij}  defined only on graph edges, and the behavioral atlas  \mathcal{T} = (\mathcal{G}, \mathcal{B}, \mathcal{C}) . We prove that  K_{ij}  is a lower-bound estimator of the graph-Lipschitz constant  L , with  \max_{(i,j)\in E} K_{ij} \le L . We establish the inapplicability of the classical differential framework on discrete  \mathcal{X} . We replace the notion of “phase transition” with a two-level falsifiable hypothesis:  H_1  (existence of local critical zones) and  H_2  (critical signatures). We specify a corrected hierarchical statistical architecture including a two-tier repetition design, a seven-phase experimental protocol with a conditional mechanistic bridge to open-weight models, and a consolidated null-calibration procedure. We explicitly differentiate our program from four closely related works: Turbulence (binary correctness cliffs in code with oracles), POSIX (log-likelihood sensitivity index), ProSA (instance-level prompt sensitivity), and IMMBA (variance decomposition without topology).

---

1. Introduction and Scientific Positioning

1.1 What Already Exists (and Cannot Be Claimed as Original Contribution)

The 2024–2026 literature has matured considerably on the question of LLM sensitivity to prompt perturbations. Five lines of work are directly relevant and must be differentiated from the present program.

Metamorphic Testing (MT). LGMT (Logic-Grounded Metamorphic Testing) is an oracle-free framework that leverages first-order logic to evaluate LLM reasoning by deriving metamorphic relations from formal logical equivalences, constructing semantically invariant test cases, and detecting reasoning defects through cross-case consistency checking. A systematic literature review by Asgari et al. (2025) studies 45 primary papers on metamorphic testing for deep code models, analyzing transformations, techniques, and evaluation methods, and identifying key challenges and future directions. Most MT work verifies pass/fail of metamorphic relations; few attempt to infer a functional topography from violation patterns.

Turbulence (Honarvar et al., arXiv:2312.14856, ICST 2025). Turbulence instantiates question neighborhoods — parameterized prompt families around a template — and explicitly identifies “anomalies where the LLM correctly solves almost all questions in a neighbourhood but fails for particular parameter instantiations,” detecting discontinuities in correctness across the neighborhood. This is a critical zone in prompt space, found with an oracle. Our precise differentiation: Turbulence detects binary correctness cliffs in code using executable test oracles; our program detects graded behavioral displacement in oracle-free, open-ended domains using response-space distances.

ProSA (Zhuo et al., EMNLP 2024 Findings). ProSA introduces a novel sensitivity metric, PromptSensiScore, and leverages decoding confidence to elucidate underlying mechanisms. It does instance-level sensitivity analysis and links it causally to decoding confidence.

POSIX (Chatterjee et al., EMNLP 2024 Findings). POSIX captures the relative change in log-likelihood of a given response upon replacing the prompt with a different intent-preserving prompt. It builds 60 intent-preserving variants per prompt across three perturbation types (spelling, template, paraphrase) and defines a sensitivity index — including the subtle case where responses are identical strings but confidence varies.

IMMBA (Di Oliveira et al., 2025). IMMBA combines Linear Mixed Models with bootstrap resampling to decompose output variability into fixed effects and random effects, offering a reproducible and scalable foundation for evaluating LLMs in variance-prone settings.

1.2 The Open Scientific Question

Beyond sensitivity — already measured by PBSS, LGMT, Turbulence, POSIX, and ProSA — the question that has not been addressed is:

Can the local geometry of behavioral responses reveal a reproducible functional structure that is invisible to conventional measures of accuracy, robustness, or metamorphic consistency?

This question has not been treated by PBSS (global variance), LGMT (binary violations), Turbulence (binary correctness cliffs with oracles), POSIX (log-likelihood sensitivity), ProSA (instance-level sensitivity), or IMMBA (variance decomposition without topology).

The gap is therefore precise: no existing framework constructs a labeled graph of semantically equivalent prompts, defines edge-level sensitivity on that graph, and asks whether the resulting atlas exhibits reproducible critical zones and component-wise attribution patterns detectable without an oracle.

