2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 The Model as a Function on a Discrete Space

Let  \mathcal{X}  be the space of prompts (discrete strings) and  \mathcal{Y}  the space of responses. An LLM is an unknown function

f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}, \quad X \mapsto Y = f(X).

Remark 1 (Inapplicability of the Classical Differential Framework).  \mathcal{X}  is a discrete space without canonical differentiable structure. Neither  \mathcal{X}  nor  \mathcal{Y}  are open subsets of normed vector spaces. Any interpolation via an embedding  E: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^d  produces a point  z(t)  that is generally not the image of a valid prompt and is never accessible to a black-box API. The Fréchet derivative  Df(X_0)  is not defined on  \mathcal{X}  — not merely for lack of regularity of  f , but because the domain of  f  contains no non-trivial open neighborhood. Directional finite differences on graph edges remain nonetheless definable.

2.2 The Perturbation Graph

Definition 1 (Perturbation Family). For a source prompt  X_0 , a perturbation family is a finite set

\mathcal{F}(X_0) = \{X_0, X_1, \dots, X_n\}

where each  X_i = T_i(X_0)  is obtained by a controlled transformation  T_i: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X} , and where each  X_i  is validated as belonging to the semantic equivalence class  \mathcal{E}(X_0) .

Definition 2 (Perturbation Graph). The perturbation graph associated with  \mathcal{F}(X_0)  is

\mathcal{G} = (V, E, W)

with  V = \mathcal{F}(X_0) ,  E \subseteq V \times V  the set of edges connecting prompts whose input distance is minimal, and  W: E \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}  a weighting function. The typical structure is a graded chain per perturbation type plus cross-type edges at matched dose, rather than a star graph, to enable dose-response analysis. Only edges with minimal  P_{ij}  are retained, guaranteeing  P_{ij} \geq \epsilon > 0  by construction of the perturbation grammar.

2.3 The Behavioral Displacement Field

Definition 3 (Behavioral Displacement). For each pair  (X_i, X_j) \in E , the behavioral displacement is

B_{ij} = D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j))

where  D_Y: \mathcal{Y} \times \mathcal{Y} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}  is a metric or pseudo-metric on the response space.

Definition 4 (Input Perturbation). The corresponding input perturbation is

P_{ij} = D_X(X_i, X_j)

where  D_X: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}  is a metric or pseudo-metric on the prompt space.

Definition 5 (Local Sensitivity). The local sensitivity on edge  (i,j)  is

K_{ij} = \frac{B_{ij}}{P_{ij}} \quad \text{if } P_{ij} > 0.

Proposition 1 (Boundedness of  K_{ij} ). If  D_X  and  D_Y  are bounded on  \mathcal{G}  and if  P_{ij} \geq \epsilon > 0  by construction of the perturbation grammar, then  K_{ij}  is bounded on  E :

K_{ij} \leq \frac{M_Y}{\epsilon} < \infty.

Proof.  B_{ij} \leq M_Y < \infty  because  D_Y  is bounded.  P_{ij} \geq \epsilon > 0  by construction. Therefore  K_{ij} \leq M_Y / \epsilon < \infty .  \square 

Remark 2 (Normalization and Null Calibration). Without normalization of  D_X  and  D_Y , the values  K \gg 1  or  K \approx 0  have no absolute meaning. We normalize  D_X  and  D_Y  on  [0,1] . The null band is defined exclusively from repeated identical prompts at fixed model and temperature (Definition 8), not from semantically unrelated prompts. The standardized sensitivity is

K^{\text{std}}_{ij} = \frac{K_{ij} - \operatorname{median}(K_{\text{null}})}{\operatorname{MAD}(K_{\text{null}})}

where  K_{\text{null}}  is the empirical distribution of  B_{ij}/P_{ij}  computed from repeated-identical-prompt pairs.

2.4 The Displacement Field as Primary Object

Definition 6 (Behavioral Displacement Field). The complete field associated with  \mathcal{G}  is

\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{G}) = \left\{ (P_{ij}, B_{ij}, K_{ij}, \tau_{ij}) \right\}_{(i,j) \in E}

where  \tau_{ij}  is the perturbation type (lexical, syntactic, semantic, structural, contextual, logical).

Definition 7 (Behavioral Atlas). The behavioral atlas is the triplet

\mathcal{T} = (\mathcal{G}, \mathcal{B}, \mathcal{C})

where  \mathcal{C}  is a classification structure of edges according to the canonical forms of  B_{ij}  and the values of  K_{ij} . We use “atlas” rather than “topology” because  \mathcal{T}  is a labeled graph with edge statistics, not a topological space.

The properties studied are:

· Invariants: edges with  K_{ij} \approx 0  (robustness).
· Amplifications:  K_{ij} \gg 1  (fragility).
· Contractions:  K_{ij} < 1  (damping).
· Critical zones: edges where  K_{ij}  exhibits a sharp local increase relative to neighboring edges along a dose axis within a perturbation type.

Canonical forms of  B(t) . The following forms are fitted only when the dose axis has sufficient resolution (see §4.5):

Form Expression  K(t) = B(t)/D_X  Interpretation
Linear  B \propto t   K \approx k  Proportional response
Threshold  B \propto \mathbb{1}[t > \tau]   K = 0  then peak Critical boundary
Saturation  B \propto 1 - e^{-\beta t}   K \propto e^{-\beta t}  Plateau
Non-monotone  B \propto t e^{-\beta t}  Peak then decline Transient response

