3. Proofs and Formal Results

3.1 Lower Bound on the Graph-Lipschitz Constant

Definition 8 (Graph-Lipschitz).  f  is graph-Lipschitz on  \mathcal{G}  with constant  L  if for all  (i,j) \in E ,

D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j)) \le L \, D_X(X_i, X_j).

Theorem 1 (Edge-Ratio Lower Bound). Let  \mathcal{G}  be a perturbation graph on  X_0 . If  f  is graph-Lipschitz on  \mathcal{G}  with constant  L , then for every edge  (i,j) \in E ,

K_{ij} \le L.

Consequently,

\max_{(i,j)\in E} K_{ij} \le L,

so the maximum observed edge sensitivity is a lower-bound estimator of the graph-Lipschitz constant. As the graph becomes denser and samples more directions, this lower bound can approach  L .

Proof. By Definition 8,  D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j)) \le L \, D_X(X_i, X_j) . Dividing by  D_X(X_i, X_j) > 0 , we obtain  K_{ij} \le L . The maximum over all edges is therefore also bounded above by  L .  \square 

Interpretation.  K_{ij}  is a lower-bound estimator of the graph-Lipschitz constant. High values indicate fragility; low values do not prove the absence of fragility, since  L  could be attained in an untested direction. The maximum observed  K  approaches  L  from below as the graph samples more directions.

Remark 3. This result is a consistency check, not a deep theorem. Its empirical content lies in estimating  L  from below and testing whether  K  is stable across families, models, and temperatures.

3.2 Component Attribution

Definition 9 (Component Attribution). Let  Y = (C, R, F, U, S)  decompose the response into five components: factual content  C , reasoning  R , form  F , uncertainty  U , and style  S . The component attribution of edge  (i,j)  is the vector

a_c(i,j) = \frac{D_c(Y_i, Y_j)}{\sum_{c' \in \{C,R,F,U,S\}} D_{c'}(Y_i, Y_j)}

for each component  c . By construction,  \sum_c a_c(i,j) = 1 .

Empirical claim (falsifiable): Fragility is concentrated in specific components. That is, the distribution of  a_c  across edges of a given perturbation type is skewed toward one or two components, rather than uniform.

Remark 4 (Orthogonality of Components). If  D_C  and  D_R  are correlated, the decomposition may overestimate separability. A correlation analysis between components is required before interpreting  a_c .

3.3 Null Variance and Calibration

Definition 10 (Null Behavioral Variance). For a prompt  X , the null variance is

V_0(X) = \operatorname{Var}\bigl(\phi(Y_{X,1}), \dots, \phi(Y_{X,r})\bigr)

where  Y_{X,k}  are  r  independent repetitions of  f(X)  with the same decoding parameters, and  \phi: \mathcal{Y} \to \mathbb{R}  is a scalar functional (e.g., length, perplexity, embedding norm) or  \phi(Y) = E(Y)  an embedding.

Proposition 2 (Exact Variance Decomposition). For two prompts  X_i, X_j  and repetitions  k = 1, \dots, r , let  B_{ij}^{\text{pert}}  denote the displacement attributable to the perturbation and  B_{ij}^{\text{sampling}}  the displacement attributable to sampling. Then

\operatorname{Var}(B_{ij}^{\text{obs}}) = \operatorname{Var}(B_{ij}^{\text{pert}}) + \operatorname{Var}(B_{ij}^{\text{sampling}}) + 2\operatorname{Cov}(B_{ij}^{\text{pert}}, B_{ij}^{\text{sampling}})

is an exact identity. The interpretation of  B_{ij}^{\text{pert}}  and  B_{ij}^{\text{sampling}}  as separable components is the operational approximation; the algebraic decomposition itself requires no model assumption.

Methodological consequence. Even at  T=0 , APIs may exhibit residual non-determinism. We cannot assume  V_0(X) = 0 . The control condition is  X = X = \dots = X  repeated  r  times, and  V_0(X)  is estimated empirically. The adjusted sensitivity is

K_{ij}^{\text{adj}} = \frac{B_{ij} - \mathbb{E}[B_{\text{null}}]}{P_{ij}}

with bootstrap confidence intervals.  B_{ij}  is interpretable only if  B_{ij} > \mathbb{E}[B_{\text{null}}] + 2\sigma_{\text{null}} .

Two-tier repetition design. Variance components  \sigma^2_{\text{rep}}  cannot be stably estimated from  r = 5  repetitions per cell. We adopt a two-tier design:

· Calibration tier:  r = 20\text{–}30  repetitions on a stratified subsample (20% of families, all models, both temperatures) for stable estimation of  \sigma^2_{\text{rep}}  and  \sigma^2_e .
· Full tier:  r = 5  repetitions on all families for the main experimental contrasts.

