4. Experimental Architecture

4.1 Perturbation Grammar

Definition 11 (Perturbation Operators). The lexicon of controlled transformations is

\mathcal{T} = \{T_{\text{lex}}, T_{\text{syn}}, T_{\text{sem}}, T_{\text{struct}}, T_{\text{ctx}}, T_{\text{log}}\}

with for each type a normalized magnitude  d \in \{0.1, 0.2, 0.3\}  for the full experiment, extended to  d \in \{0.1, 0.2, \dots, 0.8\}  for typo, orthographic, and paraphrase perturbations in the dose-resolution subset.

Three-level semantic validation. Semantic equivalence is assessed by a triplet

E(X_i, X_0) = (E_H, E_L, E_M)

where  E_H  is human equivalence (audit),  E_L  is embedding equivalence (SBERT cosine  \geq \tau_d , with domain-adaptive threshold), and  E_M  are independent semantic checks (bidirectional NLI, entailment  \geq 0.8 ). SBERT  \geq 0.85  is not the definition of equivalence; it is a preliminary filter. Disagreement cases between the three levels become experimental data themselves, labeled as “equivalence boundary.”

4.2 Probe Laboratory

·  N = 200  families, each of size  n = 19  (source + 6 types  \times  3 magnitudes).
· Domains: science QA (factual), ethics (normative), code generation (procedural).
· Total prompts:  200 \times 19 = 3{,}800  per model.
· Models: Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Mistral-7B, and one larger API model (e.g., GPT-4-class).
· Temperatures:  T \in \{0, 0.7\} .
· Repetitions:  r = 5  for full tier;  r = 25  for calibration tier.
· Budget:  3{,}800 \times 4 \times 2 \times 5 = 152{,}000  API calls (full tier), plus calibration tier.

4.3 Corrected Hierarchical Statistical Model

The corrected hierarchical linear mixed model is

Y_{ijklmr} = \mu + M_i + T_j + D_k + \beta d_l + (MT)_{ij} + (MD)_{ik} + (Td)_{jl} + (Dd)_{kl} + u_{\text{family}} + u_{\text{prompt}} + u_{\text{edge}} + u_{\text{rep}} + \varepsilon_{ijklmr}

where:

·  M_i : fixed effect of model
·  T_j : fixed effect of perturbation type
·  D_k : fixed effect of domain
·  \beta d_l : effect of perturbation magnitude
·  (MT)_{ij}, (MD)_{ik}, (Td)_{jl}, (Dd)_{kl} : interaction effects
·  u_{\text{family}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_f) : random effect of prompt family
·  u_{\text{prompt}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_p) : random effect of prompt (nested within family)
·  u_{\text{edge}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_e) : random effect of edge (nested within prompt)
·  u_{\text{rep}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{\text{rep}}) : repetition effect
·  \varepsilon_{ijklmr} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_r) : residual error

Response variable specification: If  Y = B , use LMM on  \log(1+B) . If  Y = K , use GLMM Gamma or log-normal.

Inference: IMMBA with non-parametric bootstrap (1000 resamplings) for confidence intervals, and REML for variance components. Benjamini–Hochberg control across all family-level tests.

4.4 Seven-Phase Protocol

Phase Objective Duration Deliverable
1 Systematic review and gap mapping 6–8 weeks Question  \times  Method  \times  Result  \times  Limitation matrix
2 Formal experimental grammar 4 weeks Perturbation library with provenance, magnitude calibration
3 Probe laboratory 4 weeks 200 families validated at three levels
4 Multi-model experiment 12 weeks 4 models  \times  2 temperatures  \times  5 repetitions
5 Statistical inference 4 weeks Mixed models, effect sizes, bootstrap CIs
6 Critical zone detection 4 weeks Change-point algorithms (PELT, binary segmentation)
7 Mechanistic bridge (conditional) 8 weeks Logit lens, activation patching on Llama-3.1-8B

4.5 Critical Zone Detection Protocol (Revised)

Phase 6a — Segmentation. Stratify sequences by perturbation type  \tau  and family. Within each (family,  \tau ) stratum, order edges by increasing  P_{ij} . Apply PELT with L2 cost function plus adaptive penalty calibrated by false-positive rate. PELT has been shown to perform well in small-sample, high-change scenarios when combined with hybrid anomaly detection.

Phase 6b — Shape Classification. For each segment, fit the four canonical models. Honest limitation: with 3 dose levels, per-segment BIC comparison among four functional forms is under-identified. We therefore:

· For the dose-resolution subset (typo, orthographic, paraphrase;  d \in \{0.1,\dots,0.8\} ), fit all four forms and select by BIC or cross-validated predictive likelihood.
· For the full experiment (3 dose levels), restrict to a binary test: linear vs. non-linear at the ordered triple. Report  \Delta BIC with a threshold of 10 as a screening criterion, acknowledging its arbitrariness, and prioritize cross-validated predictive likelihood where possible.

Phase 6c — Cross-Validation. A critical zone is reproducible if detected in  \geq 2  temperatures or  \geq 2  models. Otherwise, it is an idiosyncrasy.

Phase 6d — Autocorrelation Test. Test residuals of  B_{ij} . PELT can overestimate the number of change-points under strong autocorrelation. Use ED-PELT as an alternative.

4.6 Behavioral Signature

\Phi(m) = (\hat{\sigma}^2_{P,\tau},\ \hat{K}_\tau,\ N_{\text{crit}},\ \tau_c,\ \alpha)

for each model  m  and perturbation type  \tau , where  \tau_c  is the mean location of critical zones and  \alpha  is the power-law exponent if  H_2  is tested. Compare  \Phi(m)  across models for clustering.

