5. From Behavior to Mechanism: Epistemological Chain

5.1 Three Levels of Claim

· Level A — Observation. The model changes its response under perturbation. Directly measurable.
· Level B — Behavioral inference. The model exhibits high local sensitivity  K_{ij}  on a specific edge. Functional hypothesis, testable by replication.
· Level C — Mechanistic attribution. The sensitivity is caused by a specific activation pattern. Requires open-weight models and mechanistic interpretability.

5.2 The Conditional Bridge

\text{Behavior} \rightarrow \text{Functional hypothesis} \rightarrow \text{Mechanistic test}

The mechanistic test is performed only for families showing reproducible critical zones across multiple models. Novelty claim: edge-conditional patching — patch only on high- K  edges — goes beyond activation steering.

5.3 Two-Level Hypothesis (Replacing “Phase Transition”)

 H_1  (Existence).  \exists X_c \in \mathcal{X}  such that  \Delta_Y(X, X_c)  increases sharply locally on an edge of  \mathcal{G} .

 H_2  (Critical Signature). If  H_1  is confirmed, the region around  X_c  possesses signatures compatible with critical behavior (divergence of integrated quantities, power law).

Falsifiability.  H_1  is rejected if for all edges of all graphs,  K_{ij}  remains bounded and without detectable discontinuity by PELT.  H_2  is rejected if no critical signatures are found;  H_1  alone remains a valid result.

Distinction from Nakaishi et al. Nakaishi, Nishikawa, and Hukushima (2024) numerically demonstrated that the difference between two regimes in GPT-2 is a phase transition with singular, divergent statistical quantities, with critical behaviors emerging at  T_c \approx 1 . Fundamental distinction: Nakaishi studies  T  (continuous decoding parameter) on  f_T(X)  at fixed  X . We study  X  (discrete prompt) on  f(X)  at fixed  T . These are orthogonal axes of the parameter space.

