6. Claimed Contributions

Proposed Title:

Differential Cartography of LLM Behavior: Toward a Functional Tomography via Controlled Semantic Perturbations

Four contributions:

1. Formal framework: Perturbation graph  \mathcal{G} , displacement field  \mathcal{B} , local sensitivity  K_{ij} , behavioral atlas  \mathcal{T} = (\mathcal{G}, \mathcal{B}, \mathcal{C}) .
2. Dataset: Benchmark of  3{,}800+  perturbation instances validated at three levels, 6 types, 3 domains, with a dose-resolution subset.
3. Behavioral signature:  \Phi(m)  per model and perturbation type — a new composite metric.
4. Empirical evidence: Detection of model-specific critical zones and component-wise sensitivity patterns.

What we do not claim: Discovery of internal mechanisms from an API alone. The term “tomography” is retained as the program name, but not as a claim of a solved inverse problem.

---

7. Limitations and Falsifiability

· Semantic equivalence is imperfect. The triplet  (E_H, E_L, E_M)  reduces but does not eliminate false positives. Krippendorff  \alpha  is required.
· API non-determinism is irreducible.  V_0(X)  is estimated but never zero. The  u_{\text{rep}}  effect captures residual variance.
· The graph-Lipschitz hypothesis may fail. If  f  is discontinuous,  K_{ij}  may diverge. This is an empirical result, not a failure.
· Generalization is bounded. Signatures are specific to the model version; updates may invalidate them.
·  H_2  is risky. If no critical signature is found,  H_1  alone remains a valid result.
· The 2026 literature risk is real. Phase 1 is imperative: verify whether “behavioral atlas” is already covered under sensitivity profile or metamorphic landscape.
· PELT is sensitive to autocorrelation. Test residuals; use ED-PELT as an alternative.
· Dose-axis resolution is limited. The full experiment has 3 dose levels; shape classification is restricted to a binary linear vs. non-linear test.

---

8. Conclusion

We have corrected the mathematical insufficiencies of V2.2: abandonment of the tautological local Lipschitz formulation, replacement of “behavioral topology” by “behavioral atlas,” consolidation of the null calibration into a single statistic built on repeated-identical-prompts, resolution of the dose/PELT contradiction through stratified segmentation and honest limitation reporting, adoption of a two-tier repetition design, deletion of the Proposition 3 hedge and replacement of the vacuous Proposition 2 by a measurable attribution definition, and explicit differentiation from Turbulence, POSIX, ProSA, and IMMBA.

The program is defensible because it formulates precise and falsifiable claims about the behavioral properties of LLMs while explicitly refusing to overinterpret API observations as mechanistic evidence. The next step — imperative before any experimentation — is Phase 1: systematic review and gap mapping, with particularly severe scrutiny of the hypothesis that the 2026 literature has already constructed this cartography under a different terminology.


