Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations and Detailed Proofs

A.1 Notation and Conventions

Throughout,  \mathcal{X}  denotes the space of prompts (finite strings over a vocabulary  \Sigma ), and  \mathcal{Y}  denotes the space of responses. For a source prompt  X_0 \in \mathcal{X} , a perturbation family is a finite set

\mathcal{F}(X_0) = \{X_0, X_1, \dots, X_n\} \subset \mathcal{X},

where each  X_i = T_i(X_0)  for a controlled transformation  T_i . The perturbation graph is  \mathcal{G} = (V, E, W)  with  V = \mathcal{F}(X_0) . For each edge  (i,j) \in E , we define:

P_{ij} = D_X(X_i, X_j), \qquad B_{ij} = D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j)), \qquad K_{ij} = \frac{B_{ij}}{P_{ij}}.

We assume  D_X  and  D_Y  are bounded on  \mathcal{G} :

\sup_{X \in V} \operatorname{diam}_X(V) = M_X < \infty, \qquad \sup_{Y \in f(V)} \operatorname{diam}_Y(f(V)) = M_Y < \infty.

A.2 Proof of Proposition 1 (Boundedness of  K_{ij} )

Proposition A.1. If  D_Y  is bounded on  \mathcal{G}  with  M_Y = \sup_{(i,j) \in E} D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j)) < \infty , and if  P_{ij} \geq \epsilon > 0  for all  (i,j) \in E  by construction of the perturbation grammar, then

K_{ij} \leq \frac{M_Y}{\epsilon} < \infty.

Proof. By definition,  B_{ij} = D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j)) \leq M_Y  for all  (i,j) \in E . Since  P_{ij} \geq \epsilon > 0 ,

K_{ij} = \frac{B_{ij}}{P_{ij}} \leq \frac{M_Y}{\epsilon} < \infty.

The bound is uniform over  E .  \square 

Corollary A.1. If  D_X  and  D_Y  are normalized so that  D_X, D_Y \in [0,1] , then  M_Y \leq 1  and

K_{ij} \leq \frac{1}{\epsilon}.

A.3 Proof of Theorem 1 (Edge-Ratio Lower Bound)

Definition A.1 (Graph-Lipschitz). A function  f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}  is graph-Lipschitz on  \mathcal{G} = (V, E, W)  with constant  L \geq 0  if for all  (i,j) \in E ,

D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j)) \leq L \, D_X(X_i, X_j).

Theorem A.1 (Edge-Ratio Lower Bound). Let  \mathcal{G}  be a perturbation graph on  X_0 . If  f  is graph-Lipschitz on  \mathcal{G}  with constant  L , then for every edge  (i,j) \in E ,

K_{ij} \leq L.

Consequently,

\max_{(i,j) \in E} K_{ij} \leq L,

so the maximum observed edge sensitivity is a lower-bound estimator of the graph-Lipschitz constant  L .

Proof. By Definition A.1,

D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j)) \leq L \, D_X(X_i, X_j).

Dividing both sides by  D_X(X_i, X_j) > 0 , we obtain

K_{ij} = \frac{D_Y(f(X_i), f(X_j))}{D_X(X_i, X_j)} \leq L.

Since this holds for every edge  (i,j) \in E , taking the maximum over  E  yields

\max_{(i,j) \in E} K_{ij} \leq L.

 \square 

Remark A.1. The theorem is a consistency result rather than a deep structural theorem: if  f  is graph-Lipschitz with constant  L , then no observed edge ratio can exceed  L . The empirical content lies in estimating  L  from below and testing whether the observed maximum  K  is stable across families, models, and temperatures.

Remark A.2 (Approximation of  L ). Let  E_n  be a sequence of edge sets with  \max_{(i,j) \in E_n} P_{ij} \to 0 . If  f  is continuous in the graph metric, then

\lim_{n \to \infty} \max_{(i,j) \in E_n} K_{ij} = L

provided the edges sample all directions of variation. In finite samples,  \max_E K_{ij} \leq L , and the gap  L - \max_E K_{ij}  measures the unobserved directions of maximal sensitivity.

A.4 Proof of Proposition 2 (Exact Variance Decomposition)

Proposition A.2 (Exact Variance Decomposition). Let  A  and  B  be real-valued random variables representing the perturbation-induced displacement  B_{ij}^{\text{pert}}  and the sampling-induced displacement  B_{ij}^{\text{sampling}} , respectively. Then

\operatorname{Var}(A + B) = \operatorname{Var}(A) + \operatorname{Var}(B) + 2\operatorname{Cov}(A, B)

is an exact identity.

Proof. By definition,

\operatorname{Var}(A + B) = \mathbb{E}\bigl[(A + B)^2\bigr] - \bigl(\mathbb{E}[A + B]\bigr)^2.

Expanding,

\mathbb{E}[(A + B)^2] = \mathbb{E}[A^2] + 2\mathbb{E}[AB] + \mathbb{E}[B^2],

and

(\mathbb{E}[A + B])^2 = (\mathbb{E}[A])^2 + 2\mathbb{E}[A]\mathbb{E}[B] + (\mathbb{E}[B])^2.

Subtracting,

\operatorname{Var}(A + B) = \bigl(\mathbb{E}[A^2] - (\mathbb{E}[A])^2\bigr) + \bigl(\mathbb{E}[B^2] - (\mathbb{E}[B])^2\bigr) + 2\bigl(\mathbb{E}[AB] - \mathbb{E}[A]\mathbb{E}[B]\bigr).

The first two terms are  \operatorname{Var}(A)  and  \operatorname{Var}(B) ; the third is  2\operatorname{Cov}(A, B) .  \square 

Remark A.3. The algebraic identity requires no model assumption. The interpretation of  A  and  B  as separable components of the observed displacement is the operational approximation.

A.5 Hamming Distance on Prompt Space

Definition A.2 (Hamming Distance). For two strings  X, X' \in \Sigma^n  of equal length  n , the Hamming distance is

d_H(X, X') = \sum_{k=1}^n \mathbb{1}[X_k \neq X'_k],

where  X_k  denotes the  k -th token of  X . The normalized Hamming distance is

\bar{d}_H(X, X') = \frac{d_H(X, X')}{n} \in [0,1].

Proposition A.3.  \bar{d}_H  is a metric on  \Sigma^n .

Proof. (i)  \bar{d}_H(X, X') \geq 0  with equality iff  X = X' . (ii) Symmetry:  \bar{d}_H(X, X') = \bar{d}_H(X', X) . (iii) Triangle inequality: for  X, X', X'' \in \Sigma^n ,

d_H(X, X'') = \sum_{k=1}^n \mathbb{1}[X_k \neq X''_k] \leq \sum_{k=1}^n \bigl(\mathbb{1}[X_k \neq X'_k] + \mathbb{1}[X'_k \neq X''_k]\bigr) = d_H(X, X') + d_H(X', X'').

Dividing by  n  preserves the inequality.  \square 

Remark A.4. Hamming distance is defined only for strings of equal length. For prompts of unequal length, we use a weighted variant:

d_H^w(X, X') = \sum_{k=1}^{\max(|X|, |X'|)} w_k \mathbb{1}[X_k \neq X'_k],

with weights  w_k \geq 0  and  \sum_k w_k = 1 , padding the shorter string with a null symbol.

A.6 Embedding-Based Distance and SBERT

Definition A.3 (Embedding Distance). Let  E: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^d  be a sentence embedding function (e.g., SBERT). The embedding distance is

d_E(X, X') = 1 - \cos(E(X), E(X')) = 1 - \frac{\langle E(X), E(X') \rangle}{\|E(X)\|_2 \|E(X')\|_2}.

Proposition A.4.  d_E(X, X') \in [0,2] , with  d_E = 0  iff  E(X)  and  E(X')  are collinear with the same orientation.

Proof. By Cauchy–Schwarz,  \langle E(X), E(X') \rangle \leq \|E(X)\|_2 \|E(X')\|_2 , so  \cos \in [-1,1]  and  d_E \in [0,2] . Equality  d_E = 0  holds iff  \cos = 1 , i.e.,  E(X) = \lambda E(X')  with  \lambda > 0 .  \square 

A.7 Null Calibration and Robust Standardization

Definition A.4 (Null Distribution). For a fixed model  m  and temperature  T , the null sensitivity distribution is the empirical distribution of

K_{\text{null}} = \left\{ \frac{D_Y(f(X), f(X'))}{D_X(X, X')} \right\}

where  X, X'  are identical prompts repeated  r  times, and  D_Y(f(X), f(X'))  is computed between repeated responses to the same prompt. This isolates sampling noise at zero perturbation.

Definition A.5 (Robust Standardized Sensitivity). The robust standardized sensitivity is

K^{\text{rob}}_{ij} = \frac{K_{ij} - \operatorname{median}(K_{\text{null}})}{\operatorname{MAD}(K_{\text{null}})},

where  \operatorname{MAD}(K_{\text{null}}) = \operatorname{median}(|K_{\text{null}} - \operatorname{median}(K_{\text{null}})|) .

Remark A.5. Robust standardization is preferred over mean/SD when the null distribution has heavy tails, which is common in ratio statistics.

A.8 Component Attribution and Skewness

Definition A.6 (Component Attribution). Let  Y = (C, R, F, U, S)  decompose the response into five components: factual content  C , reasoning  R , form  F , uncertainty  U , and style  S . The component attribution vector of edge  (i,j)  is

a_c(i,j) = \frac{D_c(Y_i, Y_j)}{\sum_{c' \in \{C,R,F,U,S\}} D_{c'}(Y_i, Y_j)},

where  D_c  is the distance restricted to component  c . By construction,  \sum_c a_c(i,j) = 1 .

Definition A.7 (Attribution Skewness). The attribution skewness for component  c  on perturbation type  \tau  is

\gamma_c(\tau) = \mathbb{E}_{(i,j) \sim \tau} \left[ \left( \frac{a_c(i,j) - \bar{a}_c}{\sigma_{a_c}} \right)^3 \right],

where  \bar{a}_c = \mathbb{E}[a_c]  and  \sigma_{a_c}^2 = \operatorname{Var}(a_c) . Positive skewness indicates concentration of fragility in component  c .

Empirical claim (falsifiable). For each perturbation type  \tau , the distribution of  a_c  is skewed toward one or two components, rather than uniform.

