Appendix B: Statistical Procedures

B.1 PELT Change-Point Detection

Algorithm B.1 (PELT). Given a sequence  y_{1:T}  and a cost function  C(\cdot) , PELT minimizes

Q(\tau_{1:N}; y_{1:T}) = \sum_{s=1}^{N+1} C(y_{\tau_{s-1}+1:\tau_s}) + N\beta_T,

where  \tau_0 = 0 ,  \tau_{N+1} = T , and  \beta_T  is a penalty. PELT achieves  O(T)  complexity under mild conditions via pruning.

Penalty selection. We use the BIC penalty

\beta_T = p \ln T,

where  p  is the number of parameters per segment. For L2 cost,  p = 1 .

ED-PELT (Non-parametric variant). For heavy-tailed or non-normal data, ED-PELT replaces the parametric cost with the empirical CDF-based cost:

C_{\text{np}}(y_{\tau_{s-1}+1:\tau_s}) = \int \mathcal{L}_{\text{np}}(\hat{F}_s(z)) \, dz,

where

\mathcal{L}_{\text{np}}(\hat{F}_s(z)) = (\tau_s - \tau_{s-1}) \left[ \hat{F}_s(z) \log \hat{F}_s(z) + (1 - \hat{F}_s(z)) \log(1 - \hat{F}_s(z)) \right].

ED-PELT has been shown to outperform competitor methods when mildly deviating from the normal distribution assumption.

Power considerations. For BIC-based change-point detection,  \geq 80\%  power for a single change-point is achieved only at  n \geq 30  with effect size  \geq 2.0 ; detecting 2–3 change-points requires  n \geq 50 . With 3–10 dose levels per stratum, per-segment change-point detection is underpowered; we therefore treat critical zones as population-level effects pooled across families.

Autocorrelation. Positive autocorrelation can lead to false change-points. We test residuals of  B_{ij}  for autocorrelation and use ED-PELT as a robustness check.

B.2 Benjamini–Hochberg Procedure for Hierarchical Data

Algorithm B.2 (Double BH for Hierarchical Data). Let  \{p_{g,h}\}  be p-values for hypotheses  h = 1, \dots, H_g  within groups  g = 1, \dots, G .

1. First application (batch identification). Compute the minimum p-value per group:

p_g^{\min} = \min_{h} p_{g,h}.

Apply BH at level  \alpha  to  \{p_g^{\min}\}_{g=1}^G  to identify groups with significant signals.

2. Second application (within-group rejection). For each identified group  g , apply BH at level  \alpha  to  \{p_{g,h}\}_{h=1}^{H_g} .

The double BH procedure controls the average FDR and maintains the power of BH.

Application to our design. Groups are prompt families; hypotheses within families are edge-level sensitivity tests. The first application identifies families with reproducible critical zones; the second identifies specific edges within those families.

B.3 Variance Component Estimation with REML and Bootstrap

Model. The hierarchical mixed model is

Y_{ijklmr} = \mu + M_i + T_j + D_k + \beta d_l + (MT)_{ij} + (MD)_{ik} + (Td)_{jl} + (Dd)_{kl} + u_{\text{family}} + u_{\text{prompt}} + u_{\text{edge}} + u_{\text{rep}} + \varepsilon_{ijklmr},

with

u_{\text{family}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_f), \quad u_{\text{prompt}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_p), \quad u_{\text{edge}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_e), \quad u_{\text{rep}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{\text{rep}}), \quad \varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_r).

REML estimation. Variance components  \theta = (\sigma^2_f, \sigma^2_p, \sigma^2_e, \sigma^2_{\text{rep}}, \sigma^2_r)  are estimated by restricted maximum likelihood:

\hat{\theta}_{\text{REML}} = \arg\max_{\theta} \ell_{\text{REML}}(\theta; Y),

where

\ell_{\text{REML}}(\theta; Y) = -\frac{1}{2} \log |V(\theta)| - \frac{1}{2} \log |X^T V(\theta)^{-1} X| - \frac{1}{2} (Y - X\beta)^T V(\theta)^{-1} (Y - X\beta).

Non-parametric bootstrap confidence intervals. Exact confidence intervals for variance components rely heavily on normal distribution assumptions. If random effects are not normally distributed, conventional intervals may have erratic coverage. We use non-parametric bootstrap with bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) intervals:

1. Resample clusters (families, prompts, edges) with replacement  B = 1000  times.
2. Re-estimate  \theta  via REML for each bootstrap sample.
3. Compute BCa intervals:

\left[ \hat{\theta}_{(\alpha_1)}, \hat{\theta}_{(\alpha_2)} \right],

where  \alpha_1, \alpha_2  are adjusted quantiles incorporating bias correction  z_0  and acceleration  a .

Small-sample adjustment. Incorporating a small-sample adjustment term improves the performance of bootstrap confidence intervals for small to intermediate sample sizes. For the calibration tier ( r = 20\text{–}30 ), bootstrap BCa intervals have good coverage probabilities under a variety of distribution assumptions.

B.4 Power Analysis for Change-Point Detection

Effect size. For a change-point at  \tau  with pre-change mean  \mu_1  and post-change mean  \mu_2 , the effect size is

\delta = \frac{|\mu_2 - \mu_1|}{\sigma},

where  \sigma  is the common standard deviation.

Sample size. BIC-based change-point detection achieves  \geq 80\%  power for a single change-point at  n \geq 30  with  \delta \geq 2.0 . For 2–3 change-points,  n \geq 50  is required. Our dose-resolution subset has  n = 8  dose levels per family; we therefore pool across families within a domain to achieve effective sample sizes of  n_{\text{eff}} = 200 \times 8 = 1600  per (model, temperature, perturbation type).

AR(1) autocorrelation. AR(1) autocorrelation with  \rho \geq 5.0  reduces BIC-Binseg power by 40 percentage points, but PELT with a standard penalty maintains 85–91% power even under moderate autocorrelation.

B.5 Krippendorff’s Alpha for Inter-Annotator Agreement

Definition B.1 (Krippendorff’s  \alpha ). For  n  subjects categorized into  k  categories, Krippendorff’s alpha is

\alpha = 1 - \frac{D_o}{D_e},

where  D_o  is the observed disagreement among labels assigned to units, and  D_e  is the disagreement expected by chance.

Interpretation.  \alpha \geq 0.80  indicates high agreement;  0.67 \leq \alpha < 0.80  is acceptable in exploratory studies;  \alpha < 0.67  is tentative.

Application. Two human annotators independently label a stratified sample of 200 perturbation pairs as “equivalent” or “not equivalent.” Krippendorff’s  \alpha  is computed on this sample. If  \alpha < 0.67 , the semantic equivalence class  \mathcal{E}(X_0)  is not reliably defined, and the perturbation grammar requires revision.

