Appendix C: Semantic Validation Protocol

C.1 Three-Level Validation

For each perturbation pair  (X_i, X_0) , semantic equivalence is assessed by a triplet

E(X_i, X_0) = (E_H, E_L, E_M),

where:

·  E_H : human equivalence (binary judgment by two annotators).
·  E_L : embedding equivalence,  \cos(E(X_i), E(X_0)) \geq \tau_d , with domain-adaptive threshold  \tau_d \in [0.85, 0.95] .
·  E_M : independent semantic checks: bidirectional NLI entailment  \geq 0.8 .

Consensus rule. A pair is labeled “equivalent” if  E_H = 1  and  E_L = 1  and  E_M = 1 . Disagreement cases are labeled “equivalence boundary” and become experimental data.

C.2 NLI-Based Semantic Equivalence

Definition C.1 (Bidirectional Entailment). Two sentences  s_1  and  s_2  are semantically equivalent under NLI if

\text{NLI}(s_1, s_2) = \text{entailment} \quad \text{and} \quad \text{NLI}(s_2, s_1) = \text{entailment}.

Implementation. We use a DeBERTa-large model fine-tuned on MNLI. The entailment probability threshold is  0.8 , consistent with prior work on semantic uncertainty.

C.3 SBERT Cosine Threshold Calibration

Domain-adaptive threshold. For each domain (science QA, ethics, code generation), we calibrate  \tau_d  on a held-out set of 50 human-verified equivalent pairs and 50 human-verified non-equivalent pairs. The threshold maximizes the F1 score:

\tau_d^* = \arg\max_{\tau} \frac{2 \cdot \text{Precision}(\tau) \cdot \text{Recall}(\tau)}{\text{Precision}(\tau) + \text{Recall}(\tau)}.

Default range.  \tau_d \in [0.85, 0.95] , with SBERT  \geq 0.85  as a preliminary filter, not the definition of equivalence.

