Appendix F: Behavioral Signature — Formal Definition

F.1 Signature Components

For each model  m  and perturbation type  \tau , the behavioral signature is

\Phi(m, \tau) = \left( \hat{\sigma}^2_{P,\tau}, \ \hat{K}_\tau, \ N_{\text{crit}}, \ \tau_c, \ \alpha \right),

where:

·  \hat{\sigma}^2_{P,\tau} : estimated variance of  K_{ij}  across edges of type  \tau .
·  \hat{K}_\tau : median  K_{ij}  for edges of type  \tau .
·  N_{\text{crit}} : number of critical zones detected.
·  \tau_c : mean location of critical zones on the dose axis.
·  \alpha : power-law exponent of the critical-zone size distribution, if  H_2  is tested.

F.2 Power-Law Exponent Estimation

Definition F.1 (Power-Law Distribution). A positive random variable  X  follows a power law with exponent  \alpha > 1  if

\mathbb{P}(X > x) \sim C x^{-\alpha} \quad \text{as } x \to \infty.

Estimation. We use maximum likelihood estimation on the tail:

\hat{\alpha} = 1 + n \left[ \sum_{i=1}^n \log \frac{x_i}{x_{\min}} \right]^{-1},

where  x_{\min}  is a threshold chosen by minimizing the Kolmogorov–Smirnov distance between the empirical and fitted distributions.

Interpretation. If  \alpha  is significantly different from the value expected under a smooth response, this supports  H_2  (critical signature). If no critical signature is found,  H_1  alone remains a valid result.

---

Appendix G: Falsifiability Criteria

G.1 Rejection of  H_1 

 H_1  (existence of local critical zones) is rejected if, for all edges of all graphs,

K_{ij} \leq K_{\text{null}} + 2\sigma_{\text{null}}

and no discontinuity is detected by PELT at the  \alpha = 0.05  level after BH correction.

G.2 Rejection of  H_2 

 H_2  (critical signature) is rejected if no power-law decay of critical-zone sizes is found, or if the estimated exponent  \hat{\alpha}  is consistent with a smooth response (e.g., exponential decay).

G.3 Pre-Registration

All rejection criteria are pre-registered before data collection. The analysis plan is deposited on OSF prior to Phase 4.

